 The production service contract and required security controls are documented in [`connector-service/README.md`](connector-service/README.md). The [connector coverage matrix](docs/CONNECTOR_COVERAGE.md) explains why this is not yet a Plaid-like production network.
 
+## Dry-run a playbook repair
+
+When a benefits page has changed and you still have that page open, the side panel can ask the Nava API to repair the playbook without a model.
+
+1. On the start screen, save the shared planner API address and a tenant key.
+2. Choose the program the way you already start an application. The repair uses that program id (`wic`, `ihss`, `calfresh`, `medical`, or `calworks`). A BenefitsCal application that includes more than one of CalFresh, Medi-Cal, and CalWORKs sends the first id you selected. The API looks up the playbook that includes it. The extension does not guess a program from the page address.
+3. On the application card, choose **Dry-run a playbook repair**. The extension reads a selector, the accessible label, the input type, and how many elements that selector matches. It does not read or send what is typed in the boxes, including placeholder text.
+4. The panel shows what stayed in the same place, what moved, what was not repaired, and what was left off the map. Nothing is saved.
+5. **Publish this repair** appears only when the API says the repair can be published. Confirm it in the panel before anything is written. Publishing saves a new version for your organization and leaves the shared playbook unchanged. The form is not submitted.
+
+If the API cannot be reached, the panel says so. It does not invent a field map.
+
